R dominates at 532ms with the global and scatter variants of the interaction evaluation kernel accounting for nearly all of that time.\n\nFor Round 0, the measured time is 179ms but the GPU kernels add up to around 647ms, which suggests significant overlap or batching happening—the constraint evaluation and logup interaction kernels are the main contributors, with the parallel variants taking the bulk of the time.\n\nNow looking at the Trace Commit phase, the measured time is 254ms with the Poseidon2 hashing kernel dominating at 231ms, followed by the zero hash calculation, merkle tree initialization, and compression steps.\n\nFor the MLE Rounds phase at 172ms, I'm seeing the zerocheck and logup batch kernels running multiple instances in parallel, which accounts for most of the time there.\n\nBut wait—I need to reconsider the overlap optimization. The logup precompute kernels are actually being run during Round 0 now, so they shouldn't be double-counted in the MLE Rounds total. Let me trace through which kernels are actually part of the sequential MLE phase versus what's been pulled forward.\n\nLooking at the actual MLE Rounds GPU time, I'm getting around 116ms from the batch_mle, monomial, interpolation, and fold kernels, but the measured wall time is 172ms. That 56ms gap is likely CPU overhead and API calls between kernel launches.\n\nFor Stacked Reduction, I'm seeing about 22ms from the block sum kernel, 9ms from the fold kernel, plus another 15ms from related MLE and fold work, with additional NTT computation and CPU post-processing on top.\n\nWHIR is showing 18ms from the algebraic batch traces kernel, and there's more work happening with merkle operations.\n\nNow I'm looking at the performance scaling across different configurations. The key issue is that Round 0 and MLE Rounds aren't scaling properly—Round 0 is basically flat at 0.98x scaling, and MLE Rounds is actually going backwards at 0.66x. Meanwhile, Trace Commit and WHIR are scaling well above 2x, whi