{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01KNfqdpMnpKzPKSrgfX9jCr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The NVTX events are only from CCCL (CUB) library calls, not from the Rust tracing spans. The Rust tracing spans are tracked in the metrics.json file, not in nsight's NVTX system. So I can't get detailed nsight timing for each prover phase.\n\nLet me take a different approach. Instead of trying to get precise sub-span timings from nsight, let me reason about the optimization opportunities from what I already know and dive deeper into the source code.\n\nLet me focus on the most promising optimization targets:\n\n**1. Trace Commit RS code matrix computation:**\n\n\nThe `rs_code_matrix` function performs MLE interpolation followed by NTT to generate the Reed-Solomon codeword from a stacked matrix. When there are multiple stacked groups with different heights, they're likely processed through separate `stacked_commit` calls. Looking at the actual prover code, I see the main trace commit operation collects all traces and passes them to the device's commit function.\n\nThe commit function takes all these traces and produces a single stacked matrix from them. The `StackedLayout` groups traces by height, stacking columns side by side with zero-padding for shorter traces so everything fits within the maximum height. The `l_skip` and `n_stack` parameters control how traces are grouped—`l_skip` determines how many height levels to skip from the top, while `n_stack` specifies how many height levels to group together, creating buckets where traces within a factor of `2^n_stack` in height get stacked together. Since the commit function produces one commitment, it must be generating a single stacked matrix from all the grouped traces.\n\nLooking at the actual implementation, the commit function passes all traces to a stacking function along with these parameters, creating one unified stacked matrix that then gets