the RS expansion pipeline, the NTT on the full buffer is clearly the bottleneck. The profiling shows the CT_NTT kernels dominating the runtime, with the larger variant taking 82ms across 50 instances and the smaller one at 30ms across 25 instances, plus another 32ms from the batch_ntt_kernel itself.\n\nWhen I account for just the main trace commits across two segments, the NTT and bit-reversal operations together would consume roughly 100ms, which aligns with my earlier estimate of 100-150ms for the full RS expansion. This puts the total trace commit time at around 176-256ms when factoring in stacking and Merkle tree construction, which matches the observed 254ms.\n\nThe NTT is clearly where optimization efforts should focus if we want to speed up the trace commit phase.\n\nNow looking at this differently, I'm noticing that MLE Rounds has a suspicious scaling pattern. At APC 300 it takes 172ms, but at APC 0 with 2.35x fewer cells it's still 114ms—it should theoretically drop to around 49ms if it scaled properly. That 123ms gap represents pure overhead that shouldn't be there. Even if I can't eliminate it entirely, getting the scaling to flatten out would save 58ms, or pushing it to 1.5x scaling would recover 96ms.\n\nBut I've already tried several approaches to chip away at the per-operation overhead—batch uploads, buffer allocation optimizations, interpolation improvements—and most either didn't stick or only yielded modest gains. The agent's profiling shows the culprit is distributed across multiple places: the per-trace device transfers taking about 1 microsecond each, the monomial descriptor allocations and uploads, and the D2H copies with stream synchronization. Across 20 rounds with roughly 1246 operations per AIR, that adds up to about 3ms per round or 2.4 microseconds per operation, which is where the 61ms of overhead is coming from.\n\nThe real insight is that the overhead per operation is already tiny—the problem is just the sheer volume. So instead of squ