ave around 17-20 rounds, I can estimate the actual GPU kernel time by subtracting the overhead. At APC 0 with 99 AIRs and ~20 rounds, that's roughly 1980 total operations, and with an overhead of about 2.4 microseconds per operation, the overhead totals around 4.8 milliseconds. So the pure GPU kernel time at APC 0 would be approximately 109 milliseconds out of the 114 milliseconds total.\n\nAt APC 300, the GPU kernel time is about 111 milliseconds. The fact that these are nearly identical is surprising—it suggests the kernel work isn't actually scaling down with the cell count as I'd expect. This points to something else going on, possibly that the batch kernels have some per-AIR overhead that doesn't decrease when cells are reduced.\n\nLooking at the actual kernel metrics, the `zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel` runs 24 times per round with an average of 1.1 milliseconds each, totaling around 27 milliseconds, while `logup_batch_mle_kernel` runs 45 times with an average of 0.46 milliseconds, totaling about 20 milliseconds. These are processing all 623 traces in a single batched launch, so the kernel work is tied to the AIR count rather than the cell count.\n\nThe total cell count at APC 300 is roughly 811 million, but at APC 0 it jumps to 1904 million—a 2.35x increase. If the kernel time at APC 300 is around 47 milliseconds combined, then at APC 0 it should scale to roughly 110 milliseconds, which matches the observed 114 milliseconds when accounting for overhead. At APC 300, the kernel time plus additional monomial work should account for the remaining time in the MLE rounds.\n\nNow I'm scaling the other kernels proportionally. The monomial kernels at APC 300 take 35 milliseconds, so at APC 0 they'd take around 82 milliseconds. The fold_ple and batched nondegen kernels would similarly scale from 18 milliseconds to about 42 milliseconds. This puts the total GPU work at APC 0 around 110 plus 82 plus 42 milliseconds.\n\nBut wait, that doesn't match the observed 114 milliseconds for total MLE rounds. I think the monomial kernels actually scale differently — they process individual traces in late evaluation mode, and at APC 300 more traces end up in that path because of diverse n_lift values. At APC 0, fewer traces take that route. Looking more carefully, I think the pattern is that rounds 1 through n_lift-1 use early evaluation with batched interpolation, while round n_lift uses tilde evaluation on the CPU.\n\nThen rounds n_lift+1 onward enter late evaluation with monomial kernels. At APC 300, the 623 diverse AIRs mean many small ones hit late evaluation quickly, creating lots of per-AIR monomial kernel calls. At APC 0, the 99 large AIRs stay in batched evaluation longer, so there's less monomial work overall. The reverse scaling makes sense now — APC 300 gets bogged down by per-AIR overhead on small AIRs, while APC 0 efficiently batches the few large ones.\n\nThe real bottleneck is the per-trace monomial evaluation in Case A.1. If I could batch all those late-eval monomial calls into a single kernel launch per round instead of launching separately for each trace, I'd cut down on kernel overhead and allocations. But looking at the actual code, the monomial evaluation already uses batching — `ZerocheckMonomialBatch` and `LogupMonomialBatch` combine multiple traces with descriptor arrays. I need to dig deeper into how this batching actually works.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7130,"cache_read_input_tokens":89723,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7130},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"da7aeca3-2690-42a7-b268-944d05bc2153","uuid":"5caf5384-0072-4b9c-aa10-e2789e384898"}
