rict_usize(trace_height);\n\n            trace_vdata[*air_id] = Some(TraceVData::<SC> {\n                log_height,\n                cached_commitments: trace_ctx\n                    .cached_mains\n                    .iter()\n                    .map(|cd| cd.commitment)\n                    .collect(),\n            });\n            public_values[*air_id] = trace_ctx.public_values.clone();\n        }\n        #[cfg(feature = \"metrics\")]\n        trace_metrics::<SC, _>(mpk, &trace_vdata).emit();\n\n        // Only observe commits for present AIRs.\n        // Commitments order:\n        // - 1 commitment of all common main traces\n        // - for each air:\n        //   - preprocessed commit if present\n        //   - for each cached main trace\n        //     - 1 commitment\n        transcript.observe_commit(common_main_commit);\n        drop(_main_commit_span);\n\n        for (trace_vdata, pvs, pk) in izip!(&trace_vdata, &public_values, &mpk.per_air) {\n            if !pk.vk.is_required {\n                transcript.observe(SC::F::from_bool(trace_vdata.is_some()));\n            }\n            if let Some(trace_vdata) = trace_vdata {\n                if let Some(cd) = &pk.preprocessed_data {\n                    transcript.observe_commit(cd.commitment);\n                } else {\n                    transcript.observe(SC::F::from_usize(trace_vdata.log_height));\n                }\n                for commit in &trace_vdata.cached_commitments {\n                    transcript.observe_commit(*commit);\n                }\n            }\n            for pv in pvs {\n                transcript.observe(*pv);\n            }\n        }\n\n        let (constraints_proof, r) =\n            self.device\n                .prove_rap_constraints(transcript, mpk, &ctx, &common_main_pcs_data)?;\n\n        let opening_proof =\n            self.device\n                .prove_openings(transcript, mpk, ctx, common_main_pcs_data, r.into())?;\n\n        let (gkr_proof, batch_constraint_proof) = constraints_proof.into();\n        let (stacking_proof, whir_proof) = opening_proof.into();\n\n        Ok(Proof::<SC> {\n            public_values,\n            trace_vdata,\n            common_main_commit,\n            gkr_proof,\n            batch_constraint_proof,\n            stacking_proof,\n            whir_proof,\n        })\n    }\n}\n","numLines":100,"startLine":100,"totalLines":199}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01KGF6qc3BpXyhuzGga3DkoS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I see the top-level flow:\n\n```\nprove():\n1. main_trace_commit (254ms) → produces common_main_commit + common_main_pcs_data\n   - One commit call for all traces\n   - commit → stacked_commit → stack_traces + rs_code_matrix + MerkleTree::new\n   \n2. prove_rap_constraints (Constraints: 885ms):\n   - LogUp GKR (532ms): gkr_input_evals → fractional_sumcheck_gkr\n   - Round 0 (179ms): sumcheck_uni_round0_polys → fold_ple_evals\n   - MLE Rounds (172ms): for round in 1..=n_max: eval → batch_s → fold\n\n3. prove_openings (Openings: 176ms):\n   - Stacked Reduction (75ms)\n   - WHIR (99ms)\n```\n\nThere's no separate trace commit after the challenge—the main traces are committed upfront in step 1, so all the commitment work happens there. The main trace commit gives us the Merkle root that gets sent along with the PCS data.\n\nSince step 2 needs challenges derived from that commitment, it has to wait for step 1 to finish. Step 3 then depends on step 2's output. The whole thing runs sequentially: commit, then constraints, then openings.\n\nInside the trace commit itself, we're stacking traces with a scatter kernel, running NTT and interpolation for the Reed-Solomon encoding, and building the Merkle tree with Poseidon2 hashing. The RS encoding step handles expanding and padding the stacked matrix, applying small NTTs if needed, and interpolating to the MLE.\n\nThe real bottleneck is whether we can pipeline the RS expansion with Merkle tree construction—ideally hashing rows as they come out of the NTT. But the NTT outputs in bit-reversed order, so rows don't become available in a useful sequential way. Plus the NTT is already running on the GPU, which limits what we can overlap.\n\nLooking at the bigger picture, segments are processed entirely sequentially by the caller, so I'm wondering if we could overlap segment N's Trace Commit phase with segment N-1's opening computations. Let me trace through how the segments are actually orchestrated.\n\nActually, I'm thinking about a different optimization: what if we fused the entire MLE sumcheck into a single persistent GPU kernel that stays resident on the device? Instead of launching many small kernels per round (monomial evaluation, fold, etc.) with launch overhead between them, a persistent kernel could handle the fold operation, polynomial evaluation, and batch S polynomial output all internally, then wait for the CPU to provide the next challenge before repeating. The downside is it requires CPU-GPU synchronization within the kernel using flagged memory or cooperative groups, which gets complicated and risky.\n\nBut maybe I'm overcomplicating this. Let me step back and focus on what's actually the easiest win rather than the theoretically biggest optimization.\n\nLooking at the history of what's worked: multi-streaming has been the most reliable pattern — processing per-AIR work across multiple threads with per-thread CUDA streams worked well for Round 0 and GKR input eval. The failed approaches were things like per-AIR overhead reduction and kernel grid tuning, which were too granular to matter.\n\nSo the real question is: where are there still single-threaded per-AIR loops that could benefit from multi-streaming? The MLE rounds have per-trace work, but the GPU kernels are already batched together. The CPU loop is just collecting metadata and building batches. The fold operation is also already a batch kernel, so there's not much parallelism to extract there either.\n\nI'm not seeing obvious multi-streaming targets left — the remaining per-AIR work is either CPU-bound or already batched. So I need to think about a completely different angle.\n\nWhat if I overlapped Trace Commit with pre-computation for the constraint phase? After the Merkle tree is built and the commitment root is derived, the challenges get sampled. But there's setup work that doesn't depend on the challenges yet — like allocating buffers and precomputing metadata for the logup zerocheck, or setting up the GKR input evaluation work items. The problem is that the logup zerocheck setup is called after the challenges are sampled, so there's a dependency chain I can't break.\n\nHowever, I could pre-compute things that genuinely don't depend on challenges — per-AIR metadata like constraint DAGs and interaction metadata, buffer size calculations, work item sorting. These happen inside the logup zerocheck initialization and could theoretically run before the commitment, though they're probably fast enough that it won't matter much.\n\nLet me look at this from a different angle entirely: what if I just reduce the actual compute work? Looking at the GPU kernel time breakdown, the biggest consumers are the GKR input evaluation kernel at 464ms, the Round 0 constraint evaluation at 291ms, and the Round 0 interaction evaluation also at 291ms.\n\nNow I'm trying to untangle the timing breakdown more carefully. The Round 0 kernels are spread across 8 streams, so that's roughly 73ms per stream, but the actual measured Round 0 time is 179ms when accounting for GPU poly extraction and CPU post-processing. For the GKR input evaluation, the 464ms kernel time across 486 instances on 8 streams works out to about 58ms per stream, but the total measured GKR input eval is around 300ms — the rest of that time is spent in the sequential GKR protocol rounds.\n\nI'm realizing the confusion: the 8 streams don't all run the same work. The 486 instances are distributed unevenly across them, so each stream gets a different amount of GPU work. If the load were perfectly balanced, each would get about 58ms, but the wall time for GKR input eval is 300ms, which suggests there's a 5x ratio between per-stream GPU time and wall time — likely due to sequential dependencies or other bottlenecks I'm not accounting for yet. stream parallelism isn't providing 8x speedup due to:\n1. CPU-side overhead per AIR (pre-allocation, launch, etc.)\n2. Memory bandwidth saturation\n3. Thread synchronization overhead\n4. Unbalanced work distribution\n\nActually wait, I'm computing wrong. Let me recalculate.\n\nWith 486 GLOBAL instances × avg 955μs per instance = 464ms total GPU time. Across 8 streams, if perfectly balanced: 464ms / 8 = 58ms per stream. Wall time = max across streams + overhead.\n\nBut the largest GLOBAL instance is 13.4ms. If one stream gets the largest instances, that stream takes longer. The distribution matters.\n\nIf GKR input eval wall time is ~300ms, and GPU time per stream is ~58ms (average), that means the streams are highly imbalanced or there's significant non-GPU overhead per AIR.\n\nThe pre-allocation optimization already eliminated per-AIR mutex contention, so per-thread time is dominated by the GPU kernels of the AIRs assigned to it. At APC 300 with 486 GLOBAL instances and max instance time 13.4ms, if the largest 10 AIRs take 5-13ms each, one thread could accumulate 4-5 of them (~40ms) plus many small ones (~20ms), totaling ~60ms per thread. But 300ms wall time suggests other bottlenecks are at play.\n\nActually, I should verify what the GKR input eval wall time actually is. Looking at the LogUp GKR time of 532ms, this includes both GKR input evaluation and other components.\n\nFrom the prealloc-gkr-input-buffers report, GKR input evals at APC 300 take 318ms, down from 532ms before preallocation. So with current code, GKR input eval is ~300ms wall time for ~530ms total GPU time across 8 streams—that's only 1.77x speedup from 8 streams, which is poor.\n\nThe limited speedup likely comes from GPU occupancy issues and uneven work distribution across the streams.\n\nThe GLOBAL kernel evaluates the interaction DAG at the query point by reading trace columns and writing outputs, which is memory-intensive for large AIRs. To improve this, I'd need to either optimize the kernel's memory access patterns or reduce the tail latency from processing the largest AIRs.\n\nLooking at what would actually move the needle, I should focus on something with high success probability, significant impact (50ms+ improvement at APC 300), and reasonable complexity. The most promising approach is optimizing the GLOBAL kernel's memory coalescing and eliminating redundant reads—the kernel currently takes 464ms across 486 instances, so there's real opportunity here.\n\nBut I'm also considering moving `compute_batch_s_poly` to GPU, which runs every MLE round and takes about 15ms total for Lagrange interpolation across 623 traces. The GPU approach would batch all traces in a single kernel call, though the savings are modest and the implementation requires keeping evaluation results on device and managing the data transfer carefully.\n\nLooking at the bigger picture though, I should focus on which component would give the most absolute improvement. Running the numbers: LogUp GKR at 532ms would save 106ms with a 20% optimization, Trace Commit would save 51ms, Round 0 would save 36ms, MLE Rounds would save 34ms, and WHIR would save 20ms. LogUp GKR is clearly the priority—a 20% gain there gets us nearly halfway to the 2x speedup target. Let me dig into what's actually consuming time in LogUp GKR.\n\nThe input evaluation phase takes about 300ms and runs across 8 streams with GPU kernels handling the heavy lifting, so each stream processes in roughly 58ms. The protocol phase takes 232ms and runs sequentially through several kernels—the round computation takes 70ms, bit reversal takes 50ms, multifold takes 47ms, and the partial precomputation takes 45ms. The sequential nature of the protocol phase is the constraint here, so I'm wondering if there's any way to overlap these kernel executions.\n\nLooking at the protocol structure, each round has distinct phases: building fractional partial sums, computing and folding, then reverting. The dependencies between phases mean later rounds can't start until the previous round finishes, but I'm thinking about a different angle—what if I overlap the trace commitment work with the setup phase of the zerocheck and logup GPU operations? After the main commitment is computed, the transcript observes those commitments, samples challenges, and then kicks off the constraint proving. There might be idle GPU time during the challenge sampling that I could use to start preparing the next phase.\n\nThe LogupZerocheckGpu constructor does substantial initialization—allocating trees, precomputing combinations, setting up data structures—but most of it depends on the sampled challenges, so it can't happen earlier. Some parts like buffer calculations and descriptor pre-computation don't depend on challenges, though they're probably only a millisecond or two anyway. I'm also reconsidering the multi-stream approach for the stacked reduction—the previous attempt failed because per-thread accumulation buffers fragmented the CUDA memory pool, but maybe I can avoid those extra allocations entirely.\n\nThe stacked reduction itself has two phases: the block_sum kernel that produces partial sums per block, then the MLE fold rounds. The problem is accumulating those partial sums without atomics (which aren't natively supported for field elements) or extra buffers. I could use atomic operations with compare-and-swap or buffer splitting, or find a way to do parallel reduction with proper synchronization using the existing buffer. But looking at the profiling data, CPU NTT reconstruction is already the bottleneck at around 36ms out of 75ms total, so even eliminating the GPU stalled reduction might not move the needle much.\n\nLet me step back and think about what's actually worth optimizing. The Trace Commit phase involves a monolithic stacked matrix, RS expansion, and Merkle tree — there's no natural loop over traces to parallelize across streams. I could try breaking the stacked matrix into column groups and processing each on separate streams,