idx * permutation_height + row) * 2;\nreinterpret_cast<FpExt *>(d_fracs)[out_idx + (pair_idx & 1)] = result;\n```\n\n### 3. **DATA STRUCTURES & MEMORY READS**\n\n**Codec Structures (from `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/include/codec.cuh`):**\n\n- **Rule (Lines 12-15):** 128-bit encoded DAG node\n  ```cpp\n  struct { uint64_t low; uint64_t high; } Rule;\n  ```\n  Encodes: operation type (6 bits), flags (2 bits), operands X & Y (48 bits each), buffer index (24 bits).\n\n- **SourceInfo (Lines 33-38):** Identifies where a value comes from:\n  ```cpp\n  struct SourceInfo {\n      EntryType type;     // ENTRY_PREPROCESSED, ENTRY_MAIN, ENTRY_PUBLIC, ENTRY_CHALLENGE, \n                          // SRC_INTERMEDIATE, SRC_CONSTANT, SRC_IS_FIRST/LAST/TRANSITION\n      uint8_t part;       // Which main trace partition (for partitioned layouts)\n      uint8_t offset;     // Row offset (modulo height)\n      uint32_t index;     // Column index within source\n  };\n  ```\n\n- **FracExt (from `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/include/frac_ext.cuh` Lines 6-9):**\n  ```cpp\n  struct FracExt {\n      FpExt p;   // Numerator (256 bits)\n      FpExt q;   // Denominator (256 bits)\n  };\n  ```\n\n**Memory Read Operations (Lines 30-62):**\n\n| Source Type | Access Pattern | Formula |\n|---|---|---|\n| **ENTRY_PREPROCESSED** | Column-major | `d_preprocessed[height * col_idx + row_idx]` |\n| **ENTRY_MAIN** | Column-major, partitioned | `((Fp*)d_main[part_id])[height * col_idx + row_idx]` |\n| **ENTRY_PUBLIC** | Random access | `d_public_values[col_idx]` |\n| **ENTRY_CHALLENGE** | Random access | `d_challenges[col_idx]` |\n| **SRC_INTERMEDIATE** | **Strided** (GLOBAL mode!) | `d_intermediates[intermediate_stride * node_idx]` |\n\n### 4. **COALESCING ANALYSIS: THE CRITICAL BOTTLENECK**\n\n**GLOBAL Mode (template parameter `GLOBAL = true`):**\n\n**YES, there IS a coalescing issue. This is likely the root cause of the 464ms bottleneck:**\n\n```cpp\nif constexpr (GLOBAL) {\n    intermediates_ptr = (FpExt *)d_intermediates + task_offset;\n    intermediate_stride = task_stride;   // stride = gridDim.x * blockDim.x = total_threads\n}\n```\n\n**Memory Layout & Access Pattern:**\n\n1. **Allocation size:** `TASK_SIZE * buffer_size` FpExt values (Line 265 in gkr_input.rs)\n   - TASK_SIZE = 65536 = 2^16 (Line 27 in gkr_input.rs, Line 214 in gkr_input.cu)\n   - `buffer_size` = number of DAG nodes to cache per row (often 100s to 1000s)\n\n2. **Thread-to-buffer mapping:**\n   - Thread `i` reads from: `intermediates_ptr = base + i` (initial offset)\n   - Same thread accesses node `j` at: `intermediates[j * intermediate_stride]` = `intermediates[base + i + j * task_stride]`\n\n3. **Problem:** \n   - **Threads in the same warp (32 consecutive thread IDs) have STRIDED memory offsets** separated by `task_stride` elements\n   - If `task_stride` (number of threads in grid) is, say, 256×8 = 2048, then consecutive threads access memory 2048 elements apart\n   - This is a **CATASTROPHIC stride access pattern** that destroys L1 cache locality\n   - Even worse: different rows within the same thread have even worse locality because they're accessed sequentially with stride `task_stride`\n\n4. **Memory Access Example (32-thread warp):**\n   ```\n   Thread 0  reads: base+0, base+2048, base+4096, ...\n   Thread 1  reads: base+1, base+2049, base+4097, ...\n   ...\n   Thread 31 reads: base+31, base+2079, base+4127, ...\n   ```\n   These 32 accesses land on 32 **different cache lines** (assuming 128-byte cache lines and stride > 128). Zero coalescing!\n\n**Contrast with LOCAL Mode (`GLOBAL = false`):**\n```cpp\nelse {\n    FpExt intermediates[10];              // Stack array (registers/local memory)\n    intermediates_ptr = intermediates;\n    intermediate_stride = 1;              // Sequential access!\n}\n```\nEach thread keeps a small stack buffer (10 nodes) with stride 1 → perfect locality within a thread.\n\n### 5. **TASK_SIZE & GRID CONFIGURATI