ss threads is balanced or if one thread is getting stuck with disproportionately expensive work. kernel times vary from 0.067ms (min) to 13.4ms (max), with avg 0.955ms:\n- Total: 486 × 0.955ms = 464ms\n- Per thread (8 threads): 464ms / 8 = 58ms average\n- But the max thread time depends on distribution\n- With round-robin dispatch of height-sorted AIRs, the load should be well-balanced\n\nActually, after prealloc, the wall time for GKR input eval is ~300ms (from LogUp GKR 532ms - GKR protocol 232ms). But 58ms per stream would mean wall time should be ~58-70ms. The 300ms means something is wrong with my estimate.\n\nOh wait, I think the 300ms includes the sequential \"lifting\" step where SCATTER-mode results are expanded. And it includes the pre-compute_logup_combinations step that overlaps with Round 0. Let me not get hung up on the exact breakdown.\n\nThe real issue is that the intermediates buffer has a strided access pattern causing L2 cache misses, making the kernel memory-latency-bound. I need to reorganize the buffer layout to improve locality—currently it's thread-interleaved where each node's data is scattered across memory for different threads, which kills cache efficiency.\n\nA simpler fix might be to tile the processing so each thread block handles a chunk of rows and nodes together, with the intermediates buffer sized per-block instead of globally, which would keep more data in L1/L2 cache. I'm realizing not all blocks run simultaneously—with 128 SMs and 256 total blocks, each SM handles about 2 blocks at a time, so the active working set is much smaller than I initially calculated. The L2 cache should handle the data for currently active blocks, though I need to verify the math more carefully. Let me shift to a simpler, more practical approach for the buffer layout.\n\nInstead of the current structure where threads access data scattered across memory, I'm reorganizing it so that threads within a warp access consecutive memory locations for the same no