{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TxrN8X2M8Nq1opD1dG177U","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Warp-Tiled GKR Intermediates Buffer Layout\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tImprove GPU cache locality in the GKR input evaluation GLOBAL-mode kernel by reorganizing the intermediates buffer from a thread-interleaved layout to a warp-tiled layout. This targets the single largest GPU kernel (`evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel<true>`, 464ms total GPU time, 486 instances at APC 300) which currently suffers from systematic L1/L2 cache misses due to a stride-65536 access pattern in its DAG node cache.\n6\t\n7\t## Current Code Path\n8\t\n9\t### Call chain\n10\t\n11\t1. `log_gkr_input_evals()` at `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs:215` — orchestrates multi-stream dispatch\n12\t2. `process_gkr_input_air()` at `gkr_input.rs:100` — processes one AIR on one OS thread\n13\t3. `logup_gkr_input_eval()` FFI wrapper at `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs` — safe Rust wrapper\n14\t4. `_logup_gkr_input_eval()` C launcher at `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.cu:216` — launches CUDA kernel\n15\t5. `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel<true>` at `gkr_input.cu:65` — the kernel itself\n16\t\n17\t### Current intermediates layout\n18\t\n19\tThe GLOBAL-mode intermediates buffer is allocated per-thread at `gkr_input.rs:308`:\n20\t```rust\n21\tintermediates: DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(max_intermediates_len),\n22\t// where max_intermediates_len = TASK_SIZE * max_buffer_size = 65536 * B\n23\t```\n24\t\n25\tIn the kernel (`gkr_input.cu:80-87`), each CUDA thread computes its pointer into the shared buffer:\n26\t```cpp\n27\tuint32_t task_offset = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;  // thread's global ID\n28\tuint32_t task_stride = gridDim.x * blockDim.x;                 // = 65536 (TASK_SIZE)\n29\tintermediates_ptr = (FpExt *)d_intermediates + task_offset;\n30\tintermediate_stride = task_stride;  // = 65536\n31\t```\n32\t\n33\tDAG nodes are accessed as `intermediates_ptr[node_idx * intermediate_stride]`, meaning:\n34\t- Thread `i` reads node `j` at address: `base + i + j * 65536`\n35\t- Within a warp (32 consecutive threads), all access the same node `j` at consecutive addresses → **coalesced** for a single node\n36\t- But within a single thread, consecutive nodes `j` and `j+1` are **65536 × 16 = 1MB apart** → every node-to-node access misses L1 and likely L2\n37\t\n38\t### Why this is slow\n39\t\n40\t- **Per-warp working set**: `buffer_size × 65536 × 16 bytes` ≈ 100 × 65536 × 16 ≈ **100MB** — far exceeds L1 (128KB) and L2 (72MB)\n41\t- **L1 hit rate**: ~0% (working set >> L1 capacity)\n42\t- **L2 hit rate**: ~72% (100MB vs 72MB L2) — 28% of accesses go to DRAM\n43\t- Each DAG evaluation reads/writes intermediates ~2× per node (read source + write result if buffered)\n44\t- With ~100 nodes per row and 65536 concurrent threads, this generates enormous memory traffic\n45\t\n46\t### Grid configuration\n47\t\n48\t- TASK_SIZE = 65536 = 2^16 (`gkr_input.cu:214`, `gkr_input.rs:27`)\n49\t- Block size = 256 threads\n50\t- Grid size = 256 blocks\n51\t- Total threads = 65536\n52\t- `num_rows_per_tile = ceil(height / 65536)` — each thread processes 1+ rows\n53\t\n54\t## Changes\n55\t\n56\t### 1. Add `buffer_size` parameter to CUDA kernel and launcher\n57\t\n58\t**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.cu`\n59\t\n60\tAdd `uint32_t buffer_size` parameter to `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel`:\n61\t\n62\t```cpp\n63\ttemplate <bool GLOBAL>\n64\t__global__ void evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel(\n65\t    FracExt *__restrict__ d_fracs,\n66\t    // ... existing params ...\n67\t    const uint32_t num_rows_per_tile,\n68\t    const uint32_t buffer_size  // NEW\n69\t)\n70\t```\n71\t\n72\tAdd `uint32_t buffer_size` parameter to `_logup_gkr_input_eval` launcher (`gkr_input.cu:216`), pass through to kernel call (`gkr_input.cu:234`, `gkr_input.cu:249`). For the `GLOBAL=false` template instantiation, `buffer_size` is unused but still passed (consistent signature).\n73\t\n74\t### 2. Change GLOBAL intermediates layout in kernel\n75\t\n76\t**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.cu`, lines 80-92\n77\t\n78\tReplace the current GLOBAL pointer setup:\n79\t\n80\t```cpp\n81\t// BEFORE:\n82\tuint32_t task_offset = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\n83\tuint32_t task_stride = gridDim.x * blockDim.x;\n84\tif constexpr (GLOBAL) {\n85\t    intermediates_ptr = (FpExt *)d_intermediates + task_offset;\n86\t    intermediate_stride = task_stride;\n87\t}\n88\t```\n89\t\n90\tWith warp-tiled layout (note: `WARP_SIZE` is available from `launcher.cuh`):\n91\t\n92\t```cpp\n93\t// AFTER:\n94\tuint32_t task_offset = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\n95\tuint32_t task_stride = gridDim.x * blockDim.x;\n96\tif constexpr (GLOBAL) {\n97\t    uint32_t warp_id = task_offset / WARP_SIZE;\n98\t    uint32_t lane_id = task_offset % WARP_SIZE;\n99\t    intermediates_ptr = (FpExt *)d_intermediates + warp_id * buffer_size * WARP_SIZE + lane_id;\n100\t    intermediate_stride = WARP_SIZE;\n101\t}\n102\t```\n103\t\n104\tNote: `WARP_SIZE` (= 32) is already defined in `launcher.cuh` as `static const size_t WARP_SIZE = 32;`. Use it directly rather than introducing a new constant.\n105\t\n106\tThe `task_offset` and `task_stride` variables are unchanged — they're still used for the row-processing loop:\n107\t```cpp\n108\tuint32_t row = task_offset + j * task_stride;  // unchanged\n109\t```\n110\t\n111\tThe DAG evaluation helper (`evaluate_dag_entry_gkr`) and the inner loops access intermediates via `intermediates_ptr[node * intermediate_stride]`, which now reads:\n112\t- `base + warp_id * B * 32 + lane_id + node * 32`\n113\t- For a warp: 32 consecutive threads access `warp_id * B * 32 + [0..31] + node * 32` → coalesced (same as before)\n114\t- For a thread: consecutive nodes are `32 * 16 = 512 bytes` apart → **fits in L1 cache lines**\n115\t\n116\t### 3. Update Rust FFI binding\n117\t\n118\t**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`\n119\t\n120\tAdd `buffer_size: u32` to the `_logup_gkr_input_eval` extern declaration (around line 278). Update the safe wrapper `logup_gkr_input_eval()` to accept and pass `buffer_size`.\n121\t\n122\t### 4. Update Rust call site\n123\t\n124\t**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`, line 174\n125\t\n126\tPass `buffer_size as u32` to the `logup_gkr_input_eval()` call. The value is already available as `rules.inner.buffer_size` (line 158).\n127\t\n128\t## Memory Layout Comparison\n129\t\n130\t### Current (thread-interleaved, stride = 65536)\n131\t\n132\t```\n133\tMemory: [t0_n0, t1_n0, ..., t65535_n0, t0_n1, t1_n1, ..., t65535_n1, ...]\n134\t         |<---- 65536 elements ---->|  |<---- 65536 elements ---->|\n135\t```\n136\t\n137\tThread 0 accesses nodes at offsets: 0, 65536, 131072, ... (1MB apart)\n138\t\n139\t### Proposed (warp-tiled, stride = 32)\n140\t\n141\t```\n142\tMemory: [w0: t0_n0..t31_n0, t0_n1..t31_n1, ..., t0_nB..t31_nB] [w1: ...] [w2: ...] ...\n143\t         |<---- 32 * buffer_size elements per warp ---->|\n144\t```\n145\t\n146\tThread 0 accesses nodes at offsets: 0, 32, 64, ... (512 bytes apart)\n147\tPer-warp contiguous block: `buffer_size × 32 × 16` bytes ≈ **50KB** (for buffer_size=100)\n148\t\n149\t### Why the new layout is faster\n150\t\n151\tThe primary performance mechanisms are:\n152\t\n153\t1. **TLB pressure reduction.** With stride 1MB, every intermediates access touches a new 4KB page, causing a TLB miss. With stride 512B, ~8 consecutive node accesses hit the same page, reducing TLB misses by ~8x.\n154\t\n155\t2. **Hardware prefetch enablement.** The GPU hardware prefetcher can detect and prefetch accesses with strides up to ~2KB. The current 1MB stride is far outside this range; the proposed 512B stride is within it, allowing the prefetcher to hide memory latency.\n156\t\n157\t3. **L2 sector-level efficiency.** L2 cache lines are 128 bytes. With 1MB stride, each cache line loaded is used once and then evicted. With 512B stride, a thread's consecutive node accesses are close enough (~4 cache lines apart) that lines loaded for node `j` may still be resident when node `j+1` is accessed, especially for the write-then-read pattern of buffered intermediates.\n158\t\n159\t4. **Temporal reuse across rows.** When `num_rows_per_tile > 1`, the same intermediates addresses are overwritten for each row iteration. With warp-tiled layout, the per-warp 50KB working set has a chance of surviving in L2 between row iterations. With the current 100MB footprint, it's guaranteed to be evicted.\n160\t\n161\t| Metric | Current | Proposed |\n162\t|--------|---------|----------|\n163\t| Inter-node stride per thread | 1MB (65536 × 16B) | 512B (32 × 16B) |\n164\t| Per-warp contiguous working set | ~100MB (scattered) | ~50KB (contiguous) |\n165\t| TLB pages per 100-node DAG eval | ~100 new pages | ~13 new pages |\n166\t| Hardware prefetch | Disabled (stride > 2KB) | Enabled (stride = 512B) |\n167\t\n168\t## Invariants\n169\t\n170\t1. **Buffer capacity unchanged.** Total allocation remains `TASK_SIZE * buffer_size` FpExt elements. Only the mapping from (warp, node, lane) to memory address changes.\n171\t2. **No cross-thread intermediates access.** Each thread reads/writes only its own scratch region. The layout change doesn't affect data sharing.\n172\t3. **SCATTER mode (`GLOBAL=false`) unaffected.** Uses local stack array with stride 1. The `buffer_size` parameter is passed but unused.\n173\t4. **Row-processing loop unchanged.** `task_offset` and `task_stride` still govern which rows each thread processes.\n174\t5. **DAG evaluation semantics unchanged.** Same nodes, same operations, same results. Only the physical memory addresses change.\n175\t6. **Coalescing for same-node warp access preserved.** Threads 0..31 still access consecutive addresses for a given node (offset `[0..31]` within the warp tile).\n176\t7. **Output buffer unchanged.** Writes to `d_fracs` are independent of intermediates layout.\n177\t8. **Proof correctness preserved.** The kernel produces identical (p, q) fractional pairs for all AIRs.\n178\t\n179\t## Measurement Plan\n180\t\n181\t### Commands\n182\t\n183\t```bash\n184\t# In the powdr repo:\n185\tcd /home/georg/powdr/results/pairing\n186\t\n187\t# Build\n188\tcargo build --bin powdr_openvm_riscv -r --features \"metrics,cuda\"\n189\t\n190\t# APC 300 (primary target — run 3x for median)\n191\tRUST_LOG=info ./target/release/powdr_openvm_riscv prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics apc300/metrics.json --recursion\n192\t\n193\t# APC 0 (regression check)\n194\tRUST_LOG=info ./target/release/powdr_openvm_riscv prove --artifact apc000.cbor --input 0 --metrics apc000/metrics.json --recursion\n195\t\n196\t# nsight profiling (APC 300 — verify kernel time reduction)\n197\tnsys profile --output nsys_apc300 --force-overwrite true --trace cuda,nvtx,osrt --sample none --stats true -- ./target/release/powdr_openvm_riscv prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --recursion\n198\t```\n199\t\n200\t### Expected results\n201\t\n202\t**Kernel-level (nsight):**\n203\t- `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel<true>` total GPU time: 464ms → 310-380ms (1.2-1.5x speedup)\n204\t\n205\t**Wall-clock critical-path estimate:**\n206\tAt APC 300, GKR input eval runs on 8 streams per segment. Seg0 dominates (~248 GLOBAL instances, ~246ms wall time). If per-instance kernel time improves by 1.3x, the critical stream's wall time drops from ~246ms to ~189ms, saving ~57ms on seg0. Seg1 contributes ~14ms more. Total GKR input eval savings: ~57ms.\n207\t\n208\tThe GKR protocol rounds (~232ms, sequential) are unaffected.\n209\t\n210\t- **LogUp GKR at APC 300**: 532ms → 470-500ms (30-60ms improvement)\n211\t- **STARK excl trace at APC 300**: 1317ms → 1260-1290ms (30-60ms improvement)\n212\t- **APC 0 regression**: no change (single-threaded path with <100 AIRs, and at APC 0 fewer large AIRs already have good per-AIR cache locality)\n213\t\n214\t### Verification\n215\t\n216\t1. Proof verifies at all APC configs (0, 100, 300)\n217\t2. `spec.py` shows LogUp GKR improvement ≥ 20ms at APC 300\n218\t3. `nsys stats --report cuda_gpu_kern_sum` shows `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel<true>` speedup ≥ 1.2x\n219\t4. APC 0 STARK excl trace within ±20ms of before (no regression)\n220\t\n221\t## Rollback Criteria\n222\t\n223\t- Revert if STARK excl trace improvement at APC 300 < 20ms (below measurement noise threshold)\n224\t- Revert if APC 0 regresses by > 20ms\n225\t- Revert if any APC configuration fails proof verification\n226\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BvtkpxvZYQkq6Gm8hEyYP1","session_id":"da7aeca3-2690-42a7-b268-944d05bc2153","uuid":"9d09aec5-8def-4b3b-b33c-b47d0b4020a4","timestamp":"2026-04-14T11:15:12.876Z"}
